1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,924
A moss on the wall.

2
00:00:01,924 --> 00:00:03,251
A fern in a pot.

3
00:00:03,251 --> 00:00:05,079
A mango tree in the yard.

4
00:00:05,479 --> 00:00:10,085
All three have two phases, one with a
single chromosome set, one with two.

5
00:00:10,085 --> 00:00:12,135
But we only notice one of them.

6
00:00:12,585 --> 00:00:16,828
Diya asked a sharp question: when I
look at each plant, which phase am

7
00:00:16,828 --> 00:00:18,297
I actually looking at?

8
00:00:18,647 --> 00:00:20,922
The answer is different for each group.

9
00:00:20,922 --> 00:00:23,562
And it gives us three
patterns of life cycle.

10
00:00:24,062 --> 00:00:28,392
By the end, you will name the three
patterns, the plants that follow each

11
00:00:28,392 --> 00:00:30,877
one, and where meiosis happens in each.

12
00:00:31,527 --> 00:00:33,065
A quick reminder first.

13
00:00:33,415 --> 00:00:36,205
The haploid phase, n, is the gametophyte.

14
00:00:36,205 --> 00:00:37,428
It makes gametes.

15
00:00:37,878 --> 00:00:40,862
The diploid phase, two
n, is the sporophyte.

16
00:00:40,862 --> 00:00:42,674
It makes spores by meiosis.

17
00:00:43,124 --> 00:00:47,547
For every plant group, we ask: which
phase is big, green and living on

18
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:48,447
its own?

19
00:00:48,273 --> 00:00:50,065
That is the dominant phase.

20
00:00:50,715 --> 00:00:51,738
Pattern one.

21
00:00:51,738 --> 00:00:53,573
The haplontic life cycle.

22
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:57,843
Here the haploid gametophyte is
the dominant, free living plant.

23
00:00:57,843 --> 00:01:00,857
It makes gametes, and
they fuse into a zygote.

24
00:01:01,607 --> 00:01:04,190
The zygote is the only diploid cell.

25
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:06,658
There is no free living sporophyte.

26
00:01:06,658 --> 00:01:10,516
The zygote divides by meiosis,
and makes haploid spores.

27
00:01:11,266 --> 00:01:16,311
Many algae follow this pattern:
Volvox, Spirogyra, and some species of

28
00:01:16,311 --> 00:01:17,441
Chlamydomonas.

29
00:01:18,191 --> 00:01:19,176
Pattern two.

30
00:01:19,176 --> 00:01:22,347
The diplontic life cycle,
the opposite extreme.

31
00:01:22,697 --> 00:01:27,267
Here the diploid sporophyte is the
dominant, green, independent plant.

32
00:01:28,017 --> 00:01:32,628
The gametophyte is tiny: just a single
cell or a few cells, living inside

33
00:01:32,628 --> 00:01:33,718
the sporophyte.

34
00:01:34,468 --> 00:01:39,821
All seed bearing plants follow this pattern,
the gymnosperms and the angiosperms.

35
00:01:39,821 --> 00:01:41,756
So does one alga, Fucus.

36
00:01:42,506 --> 00:01:43,608
Pattern three.

37
00:01:43,608 --> 00:01:46,505
The haplo-diplontic
life cycle, in between.

38
00:01:46,855 --> 00:01:50,307
Here both phases are
multicellular, made of many cells.

39
00:01:50,307 --> 00:01:52,271
But one of them is the boss.

40
00:01:53,021 --> 00:01:56,273
In bryophytes, the haploid
gametophyte is dominant.

41
00:01:56,273 --> 00:02:00,905
The sporophyte is short lived, and
depends on it for anchorage and food.

42
00:02:01,655 --> 00:02:06,146
In pteridophytes, the diploid sporophyte
is dominant, with roots, stem,

43
00:02:06,146 --> 00:02:07,943
leaves and vascular tissue.

44
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:11,106
The gametophyte is small,
but lives on its own.

45
00:02:11,856 --> 00:02:17,115
Some algae are haplo-diplontic too:
Ectocarpus, Polysiphonia, and the kelps.

46
00:02:17,865 --> 00:02:20,523
Let's line the three
patterns up side by side.

47
00:02:20,873 --> 00:02:23,801
Haplontic: meiosis happens in the zygote.

48
00:02:23,801 --> 00:02:28,299
Diplontic and haplo-diplontic: meiosis
happens in the sporophyte, when

49
00:02:28,299 --> 00:02:29,604
it makes spores.

50
00:02:30,054 --> 00:02:32,607
Haplontic: just one diploid cell.

51
00:02:32,607 --> 00:02:35,915
Diplontic: just one to
a few haploid cells.

52
00:02:35,915 --> 00:02:39,034
Haplo-diplontic: many
cells in both phases.

53
00:02:39,684 --> 00:02:40,602
Your turn.

54
00:02:40,602 --> 00:02:43,589
A moss and a fern are
both haplo-diplontic.

55
00:02:43,589 --> 00:02:46,196
Which phase is dominant in the fern?

56
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:47,546
Pause and decide.

57
00:02:50,746 --> 00:02:51,836
The sporophyte!

58
00:02:51,836 --> 00:02:53,889
The big leafy fern is diploid.

59
00:02:53,889 --> 00:02:56,555
In a moss, it is the other way round.

60
00:02:57,205 --> 00:02:58,354
Remember this.

61
00:02:58,354 --> 00:03:00,106
Most algae: haplontic.

62
00:03:00,106 --> 00:03:03,157
Bryophytes and pteridophytes:
haplo-diplontic.

63
00:03:03,157 --> 00:03:04,962
Seed plants: diplontic.

64
00:03:05,562 --> 00:03:07,172
Now, a worked example.

65
00:03:07,172 --> 00:03:09,811
Let's answer Diya's
question for each plant.

66
00:03:10,161 --> 00:03:11,691
The moss on the wall.

67
00:03:11,691 --> 00:03:14,980
The green plant we see is
the gametophyte, haploid.

68
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:16,945
The fern in the pot.

69
00:03:16,945 --> 00:03:20,256
The leafy plant we see is
the sporophyte, diploid.

70
00:03:20,706 --> 00:03:21,880
The mango tree.

71
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,735
The whole tree is the sporophyte, diploid.

72
00:03:24,735 --> 00:03:29,182
Its gametophytes hide inside the
flower: the pollen grain and the embryo

73
00:03:29,182 --> 00:03:30,082
sac.

74
00:03:30,139 --> 00:03:33,181
And Spirogyra, the
green threads in a pond.

75
00:03:33,181 --> 00:03:34,906
Every thread is haploid.

76
00:03:34,906 --> 00:03:36,845
Only its zygote is diploid.

77
00:03:37,495 --> 00:03:38,961
Now, the exam trap.

78
00:03:39,311 --> 00:03:42,195
Many students think
every alga is haplontic.

79
00:03:42,595 --> 00:03:48,370
But Fucus is diplontic, and Ectocarpus,
Polysiphonia and kelps are haplo-diplontic.

80
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:51,041
Most algae are haplontic, not all.

81
00:03:51,691 --> 00:03:53,048
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:53,048 --> 00:03:57,026
As match the following: an organism,
and its type of life cycle.

83
00:03:57,626 --> 00:03:58,918
Exam level now.

84
00:03:58,918 --> 00:04:01,732
Match each organism to its life cycle.

85
00:04:01,732 --> 00:04:03,079
Pause and solve.

86
00:04:08,479 --> 00:04:09,855
The answer is B.

87
00:04:10,355 --> 00:04:12,424
Spirogyra is haplontic.

88
00:04:12,424 --> 00:04:14,231
Fucus is diplontic.

89
00:04:14,731 --> 00:04:20,156
Polysiphonia is haplo-diplontic, and so
is a fern, with a dominant sporophyte.

90
00:04:20,756 --> 00:04:22,217
Here is your recap.

91
00:04:22,217 --> 00:04:23,718
Screenshot this card.

92
00:04:26,918 --> 00:04:29,916
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

93
00:04:30,316 --> 00:04:31,216
One.

94
00:04:30,846 --> 00:04:34,586
In a haplontic cycle, which
is the only diploid cell?

95
00:04:35,486 --> 00:04:36,386
Two.

96
00:04:36,042 --> 00:04:39,128
Name one alga with a diplontic life cycle.

97
00:04:40,028 --> 00:04:40,928
Three.

98
00:04:40,596 --> 00:04:44,151
Which phase is dominant in
a moss, and which in a fern?

99
00:04:45,251 --> 00:04:50,079
Next time: from green slime in water
to the tallest trees, what changed

100
00:04:50,079 --> 00:04:51,270
along the way?

101
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:55,108
We find out in evolutionary
trends in plant organisation.
